  <p>Progressive overload is the idea behind almost every program that works. Here is how strength coach Jordan Hoppel explains it to a brand-new client, and what he does when it stops working.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Progressive overload is adjusting your training based on the level of improvement you are actively experiencing.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>Why the same load forever doesn't work</h2>
  <p>If you do 5 reps with 5 pounds on day one and you're stronger by week four, you should add weight or add reps. Both count as progressive overload. If you use the same load forever, your body has no stress to adapt to and no reason to improve.</p>

  <h2>Reps first, then weight</h2>
  <p>Jordan ranks his favorite ways to progress like this:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Add more reps.</li>
    <li>Add more weight.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How much weight to add</h2>
  <p>For beginners: add 5 pounds, maximum 10 pounds, per set. How often depends on the program, progress, and goals. It can happen every set, every week, or every month.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Barbell loaded with 5 pound plates showing a small weight increase" caption="Small jumps add up." brief="Photo: close-up of 2.5 lb plates going on a bar." />

  <h2>When weekly jumps stop</h2>
  <p>Assuming you're not overtrained and are properly recovered (a pure training plateau), Jordan moves lifters into training that challenges the nervous system. His first two steps:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Slow the eccentric (lowering) tempo.</li>
    <li>Pause the lift at the maximal lengthened position for 3 seconds, then push through.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What an 8-week progression looks like</h2>
  <p>Start with higher overall reps and volume (which can come from tempo too). As the 8 weeks go on, weights get heavier, overall volume decreases, and sets potentially increase. Toward the end, mix in faster, dynamic lifts to drive the nervous system. At the end of a strength program, you move light weight very fast, and move maximal weight for very low reps, potentially in different ranges of motion.</p>

  <h2>How to know it's working</h2>
  <p>Jordan's test is straightforward: if the weights you're moving continually go up according to the schedule and progression you should be following, every single week, you're getting stronger. If they stop, check recovery before anything else, and read <a href="/what-is-a-deload-week/">what a deload week is</a>.</p>
